{"name":"Tinny Tin","hex":"#4b492d","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"tinny","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of or pertaining to or resembling tin.","Pertaining to the thinness and cheapness of tin or similar-looking metals, in contrast to a heavier, more valuable metal."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative form of tinnie."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/tinny"},{"term":"tin","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Tindi."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A malleable, ductile, metallic element, resistant to corrosion, with atomic number 50 and symbol Sn.","Iron or steel sheet metal that is coated with tin as an anticorrosion protectant."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/tin"}],"entries":[{"title":"Tin","blocks":[{"text":"Tin is a chemical element; it has the symbol Sn and atomic number 50. A metallic-gray metal, tin is soft enough to be cut with little force, and a bar of tin can be bent by hand with little effort. When bent, a bar of tin makes a sound, the so-called \"tin cry\", as a result of twinning in tin crystals."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/Sn-Alpha-Beta.jpg/330px-Sn-Alpha-Beta.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tin","wikidata":"Q1096"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Tin-2.jpg/500px-Tin-2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Tin-2.jpg/500px-Tin-2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Solidified droplet of molten tin","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATin-2.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Sword_bronze_age_%282nd_version%29.jpg/500px-Sword_bronze_age_%282nd_version%29.jpg?utm_sourc